WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.470 --> 00:00:04.710
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.710 --> 00:00:07.359
Let’s talk about snow simulations!

00:00:07.359 --> 00:00:12.200
Being able to simulate snow on our computers
is not new, it’s been possible for a few

00:00:12.200 --> 00:00:19.300
years now, for instance, this legendary Disney
paper from 2013 was capable of performing

00:00:19.300 --> 00:00:20.300
that.

00:00:20.300 --> 00:00:24.239
So why do researchers write new papers on
this?

00:00:24.239 --> 00:00:28.380
Well, because the 2013 paper had its limitations.

00:00:28.380 --> 00:00:33.820
Let’s talk about them while looking at some
really sweet footage from a new paper.

00:00:33.820 --> 00:00:38.750
Limitation number one for previous works is
that their snow simulations were sinfully

00:00:38.750 --> 00:00:39.770
expensive.

00:00:39.770 --> 00:00:46.980
Most of them took not seconds, and sometimes
not even minutes, but half an hour per frame.

00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:50.550
Yes, that means all nighter simulations.

00:00:50.550 --> 00:00:56.800
They were also not as good in fracturing interactions,
simulating powder snow and avalanches were

00:00:56.800 --> 00:00:58.829
also out of reach.

00:00:58.829 --> 00:01:00.260
Until now.

00:01:00.260 --> 00:01:03.310
You see, simulating snow is quite challenging.

00:01:03.310 --> 00:01:08.190
It clumps up, deforms, breaks, and hardens
under compression.

00:01:08.190 --> 00:01:12.330
And even my favorite, phase change from fluid
to snow!

00:01:12.330 --> 00:01:17.360
These are all really challenging to simulate
properly, and in a moment, you will see that

00:01:17.360 --> 00:01:21.500
this new method is capable of even more beyond
that.

00:01:21.500 --> 00:01:23.430
Let’s start with friction.

00:01:23.430 --> 00:01:27.130
First, we turn on the snow machine.

00:01:27.130 --> 00:01:29.659
And then, engage the wipers!

00:01:29.659 --> 00:01:32.090
That looks wonderful.

00:01:32.090 --> 00:01:37.229
And now, may I request seeing some tire marks?

00:01:37.229 --> 00:01:38.490
There you go!

00:01:38.490 --> 00:01:43.450
This looks really good, so how about taking
a closer look at this phenomenon?

00:01:43.450 --> 00:01:49.549
Vb here is the boundary friction coefficient
and it is a parameter that can be chosen freely

00:01:49.549 --> 00:01:50.810
by us.

00:01:50.810 --> 00:01:53.010
So let’s see what that looks like!

00:01:53.010 --> 00:01:59.580
If we initialize this to a low value, we’ll
get very little friction, and if we crank

00:01:59.580 --> 00:02:06.850
this up, look, things get a great deal more
sticky.

00:02:06.850 --> 00:02:12.610
The big clump of snow also breaks apart in
a spectacular manner, also showcasing compression

00:02:12.610 --> 00:02:17.350
and fracturing beyond the boundary friction
effect we just looked at.

00:02:17.350 --> 00:02:18.840
Oh my!

00:02:18.840 --> 00:02:20.099
This is beautiful.

00:02:20.099 --> 00:02:24.310
Okay, now, this is a computer graphics paper.

00:02:24.310 --> 00:02:29.760
If you are a seasoned Fellow Scholar, you
know that this means…it means that it is

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:32.810
time to put some virtual bunnies into the
oven.

00:02:32.810 --> 00:02:38.540
This is a great example for rule number one
for watching physics simulations, which is

00:02:38.540 --> 00:02:42.920
that we discuss the physics part, and not
the visuals.

00:02:42.920 --> 00:02:45.180
So why are these bunnies blue?

00:02:45.180 --> 00:02:50.870
Well, let’s chuck’em in the oven and find
out.

00:02:50.870 --> 00:02:52.930
Aha!

00:02:52.930 --> 00:02:57.140
They are color coded for temperature.

00:02:57.140 --> 00:03:04.129
Look, they start from -100C, that’s the
blue, and we see the colors change as they

00:03:04.129 --> 00:03:06.350
approach zero degrees celsius.

00:03:06.350 --> 00:03:12.270
At this point they don’t yet start melting,
but they are already falling apart, so it

00:03:12.270 --> 00:03:17.599
was indeed a good design decision to show
the temperatures, because it tells us exactly

00:03:17.599 --> 00:03:19.690
what is going on here.

00:03:19.690 --> 00:03:22.349
Without it, we would be expecting melting.

00:03:22.349 --> 00:03:26.019
Well, can we see that melting in action too?

00:03:26.019 --> 00:03:27.379
You bet.

00:03:27.379 --> 00:03:32.819
Now, hold on to your papers, and bring forth
the soft-ice-o-mat!

00:03:32.819 --> 00:03:37.800
This machine can not only create an exquisite
dessert for computer graphics researchers,

00:03:37.800 --> 00:03:43.580
but also showcases the individual contributions
of this new technique one by one.

00:03:43.580 --> 00:03:44.580
Look!

00:03:44.580 --> 00:03:48.140
There is the melting, yes!

00:03:48.140 --> 00:03:51.370
Add a little frosting, and there you go.

00:03:51.370 --> 00:03:54.190
Bon appetit!

00:03:54.190 --> 00:04:00.400
Now, as we feared, many of these larger-scale
simulations require computing the physics

00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:02.960
for millions of particles.

00:04:02.960 --> 00:04:05.349
So how long does that take?

00:04:05.349 --> 00:04:11.050
When we need millions of particles, we typically
have to wait a few minutes per frame, but

00:04:11.050 --> 00:04:16.150
if we have a smaller scene, we can get away
with these computations in a few seconds per

00:04:16.150 --> 00:04:17.199
frame!

00:04:17.199 --> 00:04:24.620
Goodness, we went from hours per frame to
seconds per frame in just one paper.

00:04:24.620 --> 00:04:26.290
Outstanding work.

00:04:26.290 --> 00:04:32.690
And also, wait a second…if we are talking
millions of particles, I wonder how much memory

00:04:32.690 --> 00:04:34.900
it takes to keep track of them?

00:04:34.900 --> 00:04:37.010
Let’s see.

00:04:37.010 --> 00:04:39.699
Whoa…this is very appealing.

00:04:39.699 --> 00:04:46.620
I was expecting a few gigabytes, yet it only
asks for a fraction of it…a couple hundred

00:04:46.620 --> 00:04:47.699
megabytes.

00:04:47.699 --> 00:04:53.240
So, with this hefty value proposition, it
is no wonder that this paper has been accepted

00:04:53.240 --> 00:04:55.120
to the SIGGRAPH conference.

00:04:55.120 --> 00:04:59.210
This is the olympic gold medal of computer
graphics research, if you will.

00:04:59.210 --> 00:05:19.800
Huge congratulations to the authors of 
this paper… this 

00:05:19.800 --> 00:05:29.669
was quite an experience.

00:05:29.669 --> 00:05:47.040
What a 

00:05:47.040 --> 00:05:56.080
time to be alive!

00:05:56.080 --> 00:05:59.689
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

